WEBVTT

NOTE Two kinds of not knowing

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.970
Three people say, I do not know. One holds a die, one a sealed envelope.

2
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.033
One is asked the price of copper in twenty years.

3
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:16.210
When someone says they do not know, what exactly is it they do not know, and does

4
00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.833
the difference matter?

5
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.691
Uncertainty is not one thing.

6
00:00:20.691 --> 00:00:27.567
Two questions tell its kinds apart: chance or ignorance, and measurable or not.

7
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:35.872
Four stops: chance or ignorance, measurable or not, unknown odds, and where a case

8
00:00:35.872 --> 00:00:36.367
sits.

9
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:41.133
Start with the die and the envelope, which fail for different reasons.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:48.000
More study of a die will not tell you the next roll. That is aleatory uncertainty.

11
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.771
The number in the envelope is already fixed. Open it and the doubt is gone.

12
00:00:54.771 --> 00:00:57.400
That is epistemic uncertainty.

13
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.300
Chance stays. Ignorance can be closed with data.

14
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:10.067
So ignorance can shrink and chance cannot. But even chance needs something to count.

15
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:14.509
In 1921 Frank Knight pointed to fire insurance.

16
00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:18.300
Across many buildings, the loss evens out.

17
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.107
A new venture has no such group.

18
00:01:21.107 --> 00:01:26.567
Knight called the first kind risk, and the second true uncertainty.

19
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:34.280
In 1937 John Maynard Keynes set roulette apart from a European war, or copper prices

20
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.800
twenty years out.

21
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.794
About these, he wrote, "there is no scientific basis on which to form any calculable

22
00:01:42.794 --> 00:01:46.433
probability whatever. We simply do not know."

23
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:52.500
A number needs a group of similar cases. Without one, there is none.

24
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:59.167
Knight and Keynes drew a hard line. Ellsberg tested the ground between.

25
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:07.141
In 1961 Daniel Ellsberg offered two urns: a hundred red and black balls, mix

26
00:02:07.141 --> 00:02:09.800
unknown, or fifty of each.

27
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.367
Whichever color they bet on, most people pick fifty of each.

28
00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:22.433
No single chance of red fits that. This is ambiguity: outcomes known, odds not.

29
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:26.317
Where the odds are unknown, one number claims too much.

30
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:30.067
People treat that differently, and so should a forecast.

31
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:37.733
Chance, ignorance, no group, unknown odds: these are points on one scale.

32
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:43.697
At the far end of that scale, in one team's words, "we do not even know that we do

33
00:02:43.697 --> 00:02:44.400
not know."

34
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.400
So every forecast should first say where on the scale it stands.

35
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:54.282
Chance stays and ignorance can be closed, so say which.

36
00:02:54.282 --> 00:03:01.063
But a number needs a group of cases, and where odds are unknown, one number claims

37
00:03:01.063 --> 00:03:04.867
too much. So say where on the scale you stand.

38
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:12.145
The die is chance, with a number. The envelope is ignorance, with a number waiting.

39
00:03:12.145 --> 00:03:14.533
Copper has no honest number.

40
00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:20.618
When someone says they do not know, what exactly they do not know is a chance, a

41
00:03:20.618 --> 00:03:23.267
fact, or something no one can count.

42
00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:27.533
The difference matters: it decides what a number can mean.

43
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:32.267
The next question is what a number can honestly say.
